{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788459722056,"sessionID":"ses_f977ec03affetTdEA9a1zaz0iY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-ba779d6ec5274583","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"gh issue view float-format-research","workdir":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research"},"output":"invalid issue format: \"float-format-research\"\n","metadata":{"output":"invalid issue format: \"float-format-research\"\n","exit":1,"truncated":false},"title":"gh issue view float-format-research","time":{"start":1788459722051,"end":1788459722055}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Let me start by reading the AGENTS.md at the worktree root and understanding the task. The task is about GitHub issue \"float-format-research\". I need to:\n\n1. Read AGENTS.md (already provided in context, but I should follow it)\n2. Run `gh issue view float-format-research` and read every comment\n3. Survey how Go, Python, and Rust format IEEE binary64 floats to string and back\n4. Focus on round-trip precision and hex/exponent edge cases\n5. Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance to plans/\n6. Commit with provenance line \"Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\"\n\nWait, the brief says \"do not touch plans/\" but also says \"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance ... to plans/\". Let me re-read.\n\n\"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it -- a partial written finding beats another commitless run.\"\n\nBut then: \"Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nThat's contradictory. Let me re-read carefully.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the hard constraints: \"do not touch plans/.\" But the task says \"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance ... to plans/ and commit it.\"\n\nHmm, this is a contradiction. The ESCALATION.md guidance says: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\nWait, maybe I'm misreading. Let me look again at the exact text:\n\n\"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it -- a partial written finding beats another commitless run. While iterating, test with `just check` (the fast loop); `just --list` names every repo task. Definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green from the worktree root ... work committed on this branch per AGENTS.md with the provenance line \"Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\". Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nSo there's a direct contradiction. The task says write to plans/ AND says do not touch plans/.\n\nGiven the ESCALATION guidance, I should write ESCALATION.md describing this contradiction. But let me think about which is more likely the intent. The \"do not touch plans/\" might be a copy-paste error, or it might be the real constraint.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"Prior 6 runs re-derived context every time and wrote nothing to plans/.\" This suggests the whole point is that the prior runs FAILED to write to plans/, and the task is to actually write to plans/. So \"do not touch plans/\" seems like it's a mistake, OR it's a test to see if I'll write ESCALATION.md.\n\nHmm, but the ESCALATION guidance is clear: if something needs deciding that the brief doesn't settle, write ESCALATION.md and take the most conservative continuation.\n\nThe most conservative continuation: The task explicitly says the definition of done includes \"work committed on this branch.\" A partial written finding beats another commitless run. So writing something is better than nothing. If I can't write to plans/, where do I write? Maybe to a different location.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. Maybe \"do not touch plans/\" is the real constraint and \"Write ... to plans/\" is describing the general intent but overridden by the hard constraint. In that case I should write the findings somewhere else — maybe to ESCALATION.md or a different file.\n\nActually, I think the safest interpretation: the hard constraints override. So I should NOT write to plans/. But then where do I put the findings? The brief says \"a partial written finding beats another commitless run\" — so I must commit something. Let me put the findings in ESCALATION.md or a research file at the worktree root, and note the contradiction.\n\nWait, actually let me re-read one more time very carefully:\n\n\"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it -- a partial written finding beats another commitless run.\"\n\n\"Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nThese are genuinely contradictory. I'll treat this as needing an ESCALATION.md. The most conservative continuation that still produces a committed finding: write the research findings to a file at the worktree root (not plans/), document the contradiction in ESCALATION.md, and commit both.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the whole thing. This is a big task. Let me start by:\n1. Reading AGENTS.md (already in context but let me re-read to be sure)\n2. Running `gh issue view float-format-research`\n3. Looking at the repo structure\n4. Looking at the JS reference that's \"already-landed\"\n\nLet me begin. I'll run several things in parallel where I can.\n\nActually, the AGENTS.md is already fully in my context as \"Instructions from: /home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/AGENTS.md\". So I don't need to re-read it.\n\nLet me start by running `gh issue view float-format-research` and exploring the repo structure.\n\nLet me also check the branch name — it's `issue-float-format-research`. The issue number is \"float-format-research\" — but that's not a number. The brief says `gh issue view float-format-research`. Issues are usually numbers, but maybe this repo uses descriptive names? Or maybe it's a branch/label. Let me just run the command as given.\n\nLet me start.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06881a3c2001PeAb4orqMil8m9","sessionID":"ses_f977ec03affetTdEA9a1zaz0iY","messageID":"msg_0688140e6001GgnOmrmk5hD6Gr"}}
