{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788459736430,"sessionID":"ses_f977ec03affetTdEA9a1zaz0iY","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"read","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-988a3e56e1bf7b88","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/plans/board.yaml"},"output":"<path>/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/plans/board.yaml</path>\n<type>file</type>\n<content>\n1: - id: stuck-issue-168-investigation\n2:   kind: build\n3:   status: delegated\n4:   needs: []\n5:   issue: gh:168\n6:   title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-168 (no activity 4h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-168-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n7: - id: stuck-issue-153-investigation\n8:   kind: build\n9:   status: delegated\n10:   needs: []\n11:   issue: gh:153\n12:   title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-153 (no activity 2h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-153-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n13: - id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n14:   issue: gh:152\n15:   title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet; round 3 stress-tested each of the three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\"at least 8 times shorter\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\n16:   kind: decide\n17:   needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\n18:   prio: 3\n19:   status: todo\n20: \n21: - id: declare-terminator-build\n22:   issue: gh:153\n23:   title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n24:   kind: build\n25:   needs: []\n26:   prio: 3\n27:   status: delegated\n28: \n29: - id: dsv-partials-migration\n30:   issue: gh:136\n31:   title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n32:   kind: build\n33:   needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n34:   prio: 5\n35:   status: todo\n36: \n37: - id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n38:   issue: gh:93\n39:   title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\n40:   kind: build\n41:   needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n42:   prio: 3\n43:   status: todo\n44: \n45: - id: variant-types-flip\n46:   issue: gh:165\n47:   title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n48:   kind: build\n49:   needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n50:   prio: 2\n51:   model: fable\n52:   status: todo\n53: \n54: - id: shell-out-build\n55:   issue: gh:158\n56:   title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n57:   kind: build\n58:   needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]\n59:   status: todo\n60: \n61: - id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-design\n62:   title: 'Decide Q35 (plans/questions.md#q35): what are stdout/stderr, and does a program write or return? Two commitless runs on shell-out-build (gh:158, issue-158, 2026-09-01) both spent their full budget rediscovering that pipe_through cannot be implemented until this effect model exists, then produced no code. Round 1 answer: no pick -- maintainer''s freeText pulled two directions at once (\"well i would say real type, but isn''t it already the same types that we are using for our own program''s own inputs and outputs?\") and flagged a second open question riding along, how stdin/stdout splitting works. Needs stdout-stderr-effect-model-research (does the already-decided \"Stream is the effect layer\" model already cover a mid-pipeline write, or does it need a real Effect/IO type; how does stdin/stdout splitting fit) before a sharper round 2.'\n63:   kind: decide\n64:   needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-research]\n65:   prio: 1\n66:   status: todo\n67: \n68: - id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-research\n69:   title: 'Research spike (round 1 answer, stdout-stderr-effect-model question): the maintainer''s freeText was torn between \"a real type\" (option C, Effect/IO) and noting the program already has Stream-typed inputs/outputs (option B, extend the decided \"Stream is the effect layer\" model) -- and separately flagged that stdin/stdout splitting still needs deciding. Write up how each of the three round-1 options (opaque plumbing, Stream-carries-writes, real Effect/IO type) would actually model reading and writing on two separate streams (stdout vs stderr) using types the language already has, so round 2 can present concrete, verified code for each rather than re-asking the same abstract question. Write findings to plans/.'\n70:   kind: build\n71:   needs: []\n72:   prio: 1\n73:   status: delegated\n74: \n75: - id: stdin-redesign-build-2\n76:   issue: gh:172\n77:   title: 'Re-file of gh:159 (maintainer cleanup ruling, 2026-09-02, option C: drop the poisoned issue-159 lane, re-board under a fresh lane id rather than repair it). Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build). The old issue-159 branch/worktree are abandoned in place (permission-denied cleanup, left as garbage per the ruling); the feature diff there (commit 45c76be) is reference-only, re-derive against current main rather than cherry-pick.'\n78:   kind: build\n79:   needs: []\n80:   model: fable\n81:   status: delegated\n82: \n83: - id: trait-interface-build\n84:   issue: gh:174\n85:   title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\n86:   kind: build\n87:   needs: []\n88:   status: delegated\n89: \n90: - id: mutation-semantics-spike\n91:   title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n92:   kind: build\n93:   needs: []\n94:   prio: 4\n95:   status: todo\n96: \n97: - id: float-build-rust\n98:   issue: gh:149\n99:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Rust backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n100:   kind: build\n101:   needs: [float-build]\n102:   status: delegated\n103: \n104: - id: float-build-python\n105:   issue: gh:149\n106:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Python backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n107:   kind: build\n108:   needs: [float-build]\n109:   status: delegated\n110: \n111: - id: float-build-go\n112:   issue: gh:149\n113:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Go backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n114:   kind: build\n115:   needs: [float-build]\n116:   status: delegated\n117: \n118: - id: float-build-lua\n119:   issue: gh:149\n120:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Lua backend only (host hook), following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n121:   kind: build\n122:   needs: [float-build]\n123:   status: todo\n124: \n125: - id: float-build-jq\n126:   issue: gh:149\n127:   title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the jq backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n128:   kind: build\n129:   needs: [float-build]\n130:   status: todo\n131: \n132: - id: float-build-c-escalation\n133:   issue: gh:149\n134:   title: Attempt Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the C/LLVM backend; if there is no usable native matching primitive, write ESCALATION.md scoped to just this backend, commit it, and stop rather than stalling.\n135:   kind: build\n136:   needs: [float-build]\n137:   status: todo\n138: \n139: - id: benchmark-suite-build\n140:   title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\n141:   kind: build\n142:   needs: []\n143:   prio: 4\n144:   status: delegated\n145: \n146: - id: lines-utf8-posix-research\n147:   issue: gh:102\n148:   title: 'Round 2 answer (question lines-non-utf8-byte): no pick -- maintainer wants deeper research beyond plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201 before choosing refuse/carry-through/lossy-replace: what is most correct, most secure, most POSIX-compliant, most common across existing Unix line-oriented tools, and what would match user expectations. Write findings to plans/.'\n149:   kind: build\n150:   needs: []\n151:   prio: 3\n152:   status: todo\n153: \n154: - id: lines-utf8-edge\n155:   issue: gh:102\n156:   title: 'Pin what a non-UTF-8 byte arriving via lines does, on every backend. Round 1 research (plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201) covered per-backend mechanics only; round 2 answer asked for correctness/security/POSIX-compliance/common-practice research first (see lines-utf8-posix-research) before round 3 re-asks with the three options (refuse / carry-where-possible / uniform lossy replacement).'\n157:   kind: decide\n158:   needs: [lines-utf8-posix-research]\n159:   status: todo\n160: \n161: - id: search-layer-build\n162:   title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n163:   kind: build\n164:   needs: []\n165:   status: todo\n166: \n167: - id: sort-by-max-by\n168:   title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n169:   kind: build\n170:   needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n171:   status: todo\n172: \n173: - id: param-destructure-build\n174:   title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n175:   kind: build\n176:   needs: []\n177:   status: todo\n178: \n179: - id: bigint-tracking\n180:   issue: gh:112\n181:   title: Arbitrary-precision integers -- tracked, deliberately unscheduled\n182:   kind: build\n183:   needs: []\n184:   prio: 5\n185:   status: todo\n186: \n187: - id: js-node-web-split-build\n188:   issue: gh:160\n189:   title: Build the JS Node/Web target split, TS type generation, builtin-replacement escape hatch, and toylang.conf.yaml as one system (js-node-web-split-design ruling, gh:160)\n190:   kind: build\n191:   needs: []\n192:   status: todo\n193: \n194: - id: module-routing-syntax-build\n195:   issue: gh:167\n196:   title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\n197:   kind: build\n198:   needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]\n199:   prio: 5\n200:   status: todo\n201: \n202: - id: erlang-target-consideration\n203:   issue: gh:163\n204:   title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\n205:   kind: decide\n206:   needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\n207:   prio: 5\n208:   status: todo\n209: \n210: - id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\n211:   issue: gh:170\n212:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n213:   kind: build\n214:   needs: []\n215:   prio: 4\n216:   status: todo\n217: \n218: - id: variant-checker-capital-first\n219:   issue: gh:156\n220:   title: 'Reshaped after land-failure escalation (maintainer: \"reshape into two lanes\", 2026-09-01): checker enforcement (gh:156 ruling) is correct, but landing goes red because the doc corpus still has lowercase-leading variant examples across 6 pages -- gate/xfail the doc-corpus test `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` for exactly these 6 pages (docs/guides/enums.md:13, docs/guides/matching.md:66, docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28, docs/reference/types/enum.md:7, docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6/20/40) plus the 4 MISQUOTED fixtures (docs/guides/matching.md:54, docs/reference/types/enum.md:34/67, docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67) so the checker enforcement itself can land now. The actual doc-content migration is split out as doc-migrate-capital-variants below -- do not do that migration in this lane. Evidence: ~/.cache/toylang-drive/land-gate-issue-156.log.'\n221:   kind: build\n222:   needs: []\n223:   status: todo\n224: \n225: - id: doc-migrate-capital-variants\n226:   title: 'Follow-up lane split from issue-156 (reshape ruling, 2026-09-01): migrate the 6 flagged doc pages'' variant-declaration examples to capital-first-type + lowercase-constructor, and correct the 4 MISQUOTED fixture texts to match the checker''s real error output, then remove the corpus-test gating added in variant-checker-capital-first. Pages: docs/guides/enums.md, docs/guides/matching.md, docs/reference/operators/comparison.md, docs/reference/types/enum.md, docs/tutorial/04-enums.md, docs/tutorial/06-matching.md. Evidence for exact expected strings: ~/.cache/toylang-drive/land-gate-issue-156.log.'\n227:   kind: build\n228:   needs: [variant-checker-capital-first]\n229:   status: todo\n230: \n231: - id: match-type-wrapper-experiment\n232:   issue: gh:122\n233:   title: 'Experimentally build a Match<T> wrapper: `.` entering a `|` chain wraps as Match<T>, traits like `%` get generic impls over Match<T> per underlying T (applying only past a passing boolean guard), and an arm''s aliases become named fields on a result struct the match produces -- see what goes wrong before ratifying (gh:122 ruling)'\n234:   kind: build\n235:   needs: []\n236:   status: todo\n237: \n238: - id: closures-first-class-functions-research\n239:   issue: gh:119\n240:   title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n241:   kind: build\n242:   needs: []\n243:   prio: 3\n244:   status: todo\n245: \n246: - id: partial-application-system-design\n247:   issue: gh:119\n248:   title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n249:   kind: decide\n250:   needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n251:   status: todo\n252: \n253: \n254: \n255: - id: draft-core-model-migration\n256:   title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n257:   kind: build\n258:   model: fable\n259:   needs: []\n260:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n261:   prio: 4\n262:   status: todo\n263: \n264: - id: draft-access-model-migration\n265:   title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n266:   kind: build\n267:   needs: []\n268:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n269:   prio: 4\n270:   status: todo\n271: \n272: - id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n273:   title: Verify the Int/Float/equality sections against ADRs 0006/0007/0010 and the reference; move what's missing, delete (draft-split)\n274:   kind: build\n275:   needs: []\n276:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n277:   prio: 4\n278:   status: todo\n279: \n280: - id: draft-records-migration\n281:   title: Migrate the three record decisions (literals-and-arguments, declared order, order-not-identity) into the record reference, delete (draft-split)\n282:   kind: build\n283:   needs: []\n284:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n285:   prio: 4\n286:   status: todo\n287: \n288: - id: draft-streams-migration\n289:   title: Migrate the five stream/stdin decision sections against ADR 0001, the streams guide, and the sources reference; delete (draft-split)\n290:   kind: build\n291:   needs: []\n292:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n293:   prio: 4\n294:   status: todo\n295: \n296: - id: draft-calls-modules-migration\n297:   title: Migrate bare application and the prelude module system into the syntax/prelude reference, delete (draft-split)\n298:   kind: build\n299:   needs: []\n300:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n301:   prio: 4\n302:   status: todo\n303: \n304: - id: draft-matching-migration\n305:   title: Migrate the matching/enums sections -- ratified parts to guides and ADR 0009, codec threads to the tracker (draft-split)\n306:   kind: build\n307:   needs: []\n308:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home, matcher-model-clarification, matcher-totality-and-alt-design]\n309:   prio: 4\n310:   status: todo\n311: \n312: - id: draft-prototype-findings-migration\n313:   title: Verify every prototype-showed finding lives in research-log or is superseded, then delete the section (draft-split)\n314:   kind: build\n315:   needs: []\n316:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n317:   prio: 4\n318:   status: todo\n319: \n320: - id: draft-str-adr\n321:   title: Record the Str contract as an ADR per the string spike's recommendation, then delete the draft's strings section (draft-split)\n322:   kind: build\n323:   needs: []\n324:   soft: [draft-intro-migration, lines-utf8-edge]\n325:   prio: 3\n326:   status: todo\n327: \n328: - id: draft-mutation-migration\n329:   title: Record the mutation decisions once they land and delete both mutation sections (draft-split)\n330:   kind: build\n331:   needs: [mutation-semantics-design, binary-op-multiplicity-design]\n332:   prio: 4\n333:   status: todo\n334: \n335: - id: binary-op-multiplicity-design\n336:   title: 'Grill binary operators over multi-valued operands (cartesian, zip, or explicit) and the record-forming update''s spelling -- Q2/Q3 (draft-split). Round 2 answers (multiplicity-and-offload-round2): Q3 RATIFIED -- option B, require One on the right of `=`, forking becomes explicit; maintainer caveat: drop the `$c`-prefixed alias in the option''s preview, \"never agreed to prefacing variable names with $\" (applies to whatever alias/binding syntax eventually lands, not specific to this row). Q2 still no pick -- maintainer leans B (zip/broadcast) or C (no default, explicit) but wants to understand how much of jq''s magic/expressive power would be lost either way before choosing; needs jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research before a round 3 re-ask.'\n337:   kind: decide\n338:   needs: [jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research]\n339:   prio: 3\n340:   status: todo\n341: \n342: - id: jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research\n343:   issue: gh:169\n344:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question binary-op-default-multiplicity): survey real jq idioms that rely on the cartesian default for `Vec op Vec` (option A in binary-op-multiplicity-design), and work out concretely what breaks or gets more verbose under a zip/broadcast default (option B) versus no default at all, explicit `zip`/`cross` (option C). Use real jq snippets and their toylang-under-B and toylang-under-C equivalents so the comparison is concrete, not abstract. Write findings to plans/.'\n345:   kind: build\n346:   needs: []\n347:   prio: 3\n348:   status: todo\n349: \n350: - id: search-and-fold-design\n351:   title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needed that research before re-asking. Q7 was re-asked in the signature-matching-and-search-cut round and RULED provisional: `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible), flagged for reevaluation once recursive-descent-order-research lands. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n352:   kind: decide\n353:   needs: [search-choice-point-match-syntax-research]\n354:   soft: [first-search-cut, recursive-descent-order-research]\n355:   prio: 4\n356:   status: todo\n357: \n358: - id: recursive-descent-order-research\n359:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut round, question recursive-descent-order): Q7 was ruled provisional -- `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible) -- but the maintainer wants a deep research brief on what alternatives actually exist to the unordered/columnar-fast-path design (questions.md Q7), so the ruling can be reevaluated later with real alternatives in hand rather than the two options already on the table. Survey how other query/traversal languages and engines (jq, XPath, JSONPath, columnar/vectorized query engines) handle recursive descent order versus set semantics, and what a toylang-specific unordered fast path would cost callers who need order back. Write findings to plans/.'\n360:   kind: build\n361:   needs: []\n362:   prio: 4\n363:   status: todo\n364: \n365: - id: search-choice-point-match-syntax-research\n366:   title: 'Research spike (round answer, search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, question search-choice-point-syntax): maintainer asks for a deeper design pass on whether the choice-point (\"branch wider, explore every alternative\") operator should be spelled parallel to toylang''s match syntax instead of a new infix operator (`;`, `branch(...)`, or folding into `//`). Survey how other languages with pattern-matching-flavored search/logic constructs (Prolog disjunction, Icon/Curry generators, list-monad comprehensions) express this, and produce 2-3 concrete syntax candidates with real toylang code examples, explicitly including a match-parallel option. Write findings to plans/.'\n367:   kind: build\n368:   needs: []\n369:   prio: 3\n370:   status: todo\n371: \n372: - id: offload-boundary-design\n373:   title: 'Grill the offload/vectorization thesis -- kernel admissibility, batch invariance, parallel basis, tensors (draft-split). Round 2 answer (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility): silent/cardinality-derived vectorizability CONFIRMED (no type-level effect); the caveat''s discoverability mechanism is B now (opt-in `--explain-offload` compiler diagnostic) with C (editor/LSP hover) as a wanted future addition, not a near-term requirement. Spun off offload-explain-flag-build for B; LSP hover left unscheduled.'\n374:   kind: decide\n375:   needs: [offload-explain-flag-build]\n376:   soft: [benchmark-goals]\n377:   prio: 5\n378:   status: todo\n379: \n380: - id: offload-explain-flag-build\n381:   issue: gh:168\n382:   title: 'Implement `--explain-offload`: an opt-in compiler diagnostic that annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why -- per the offload-boundary-design round 2 ruling (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility, option B). No signature or type-system change; a build-time flag only. LSP hover (option C) is a wanted future addition, deliberately unscheduled here.'\n383:   kind: build\n384:   needs: []\n385:   prio: 4\n386:   status: todo\n387: \n388: - id: draft-md-cleanup-review\n389:   title: Review what remains of draft.md after every split row lands; assign or delete the remainder, retire the file (draft-split)\n390:   kind: build\n391:   needs: [draft-questions-home, draft-intro-migration, draft-core-model-migration, draft-access-model-migration, draft-numbers-operators-migration, draft-records-migration, draft-streams-migration, draft-calls-modules-migration, draft-matching-migration, draft-prototype-findings-migration, draft-str-adr, draft-mutation-migration, binary-op-multiplicity-design, search-and-fold-design, offload-boundary-design]\n392:   prio: 4\n393:   status: todo\n394: \n395: - id: mail-rounds-isolation\n396:   issue: gh:164\n397:   title: Mail tab must not blank on one invalid round file -- per-round fetch isolation + visible error row\n398:   kind: build\n399:   needs: []\n400:   prio: 2\n401:   status: todo\n402: \n403: - id: messagecard-flow-defensive-render\n404:   issue: gh:173\n405:   title: 'MessageCard hard-crashes the whole mail/grill UI on an unrecognized flow value (found live: two pending rounds had flow: decide, not a FlowType member, blanking the app). Fall back to a neutral style instead of throwing, and/or add an error boundary around round rendering -- the immediate bad data is already fixed in the two affected round files, this is the defensive follow-up.'\n406:   kind: build\n407:   needs: []\n408:   prio: 2\n409:   status: todo\n410: \n411: - id: http-query-sugar-design\n412:   issue: gh:171\n413:   title: 'Maintainer note (composed 2026-08-31): design minimal HTTP query support (Python-requests-like) as sugar over whatever backend already provides -- needs a grilling round on surface area before it becomes build work'\n414:   kind: decide\n415:   needs: [http-query-sugar-research]\n416:   prio: 4\n417:   status: todo\n418: \n419: - id: http-query-sugar-research\n420:   issue: gh:171\n421:   title: 'Research spike ahead of the http-query-sugar-design round: survey each backend''s existing I/O/effect capabilities (what a source/sink already looks like per backend, and whether any backend has network primitives today) and Python requests'' minimal surface (get/post, headers, body, response shape). Produce 2-3 concrete syntax candidates for HTTP query sugar as toylang code, each grounded in what the current sink/source vocabulary already supports, so the design round can ask a real question instead of a green-field one. Write findings to plans/.'\n422:   kind: build\n423:   needs: []\n424:   prio: 4\n425:   status: todo\n426: \n427: - id: brief-phrasing-experiment\n428:   title: 'Tracked in-repo per maintainer ruling (coordinator-escalation-brief-phrasing-experiment round, 2026-09-01: gh issue create was blocked by the permission classifier; no gh issue, no retry). Maintainer note (composed 2026-08-31): generic non-informational reassurance phrasing (\"its much simpler than you think\") in dispatch briefs seems to help planning agents find better plans -- experiment on at least 10 issues, then report progress and grill on the results.'\n429:   kind: build\n430:   needs: []\n431:   prio: 5\n432:   status: todo\n\n(End of file - total 432 lines)\n</content>","metadata":{"preview":"- id: stuck-issue-168-investigation\n  kind: build\n  status: delegated\n  needs: []\n  issue: gh:168\n  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-168 (no activity 4h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-168-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n- id: stuck-issue-153-investigation\n  kind: build\n  status: delegated\n  needs: []\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-153 (no activity 2h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-153-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n  issue: gh:152\n  title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet; round 3 stress-tested each of the three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\"at least 8 times shorter\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n","truncated":false,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/plans/board.yaml","text":"- id: stuck-issue-168-investigation\n  kind: build\n  status: delegated\n  needs: []\n  issue: gh:168\n  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-168 (no activity 4h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-168-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n- id: stuck-issue-153-investigation\n  kind: build\n  status: delegated\n  needs: []\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-153 (no activity 2h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-153-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n  issue: gh:152\n  title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet; round 3 stress-tested each of the three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\"at least 8 times shorter\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: declare-terminator-build\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: dsv-partials-migration\n  issue: gh:136\n  title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n  issue: gh:93\n  title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: variant-types-flip\n  issue: gh:165\n  title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n  prio: 2\n  model: fable\n  status: todo\n\n- id: shell-out-build\n  issue: gh:158\n  title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-design\n  title: 'Decide Q35 (plans/questions.md#q35): what are stdout/stderr, and does a program write or return? Two commitless runs on shell-out-build (gh:158, issue-158, 2026-09-01) both spent their full budget rediscovering that pipe_through cannot be implemented until this effect model exists, then produced no code. Round 1 answer: no pick -- maintainer''s freeText pulled two directions at once (\"well i would say real type, but isn''t it already the same types that we are using for our own program''s own inputs and outputs?\") and flagged a second open question riding along, how stdin/stdout splitting works. Needs stdout-stderr-effect-model-research (does the already-decided \"Stream is the effect layer\" model already cover a mid-pipeline write, or does it need a real Effect/IO type; how does stdin/stdout splitting fit) before a sharper round 2.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-research]\n  prio: 1\n  status: todo\n\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-research\n  title: 'Research spike (round 1 answer, stdout-stderr-effect-model question): the maintainer''s freeText was torn between \"a real type\" (option C, Effect/IO) and noting the program already has Stream-typed inputs/outputs (option B, extend the decided \"Stream is the effect layer\" model) -- and separately flagged that stdin/stdout splitting still needs deciding. Write up how each of the three round-1 options (opaque plumbing, Stream-carries-writes, real Effect/IO type) would actually model reading and writing on two separate streams (stdout vs stderr) using types the language already has, so round 2 can present concrete, verified code for each rather than re-asking the same abstract question. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 1\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: stdin-redesign-build-2\n  issue: gh:172\n  title: 'Re-file of gh:159 (maintainer cleanup ruling, 2026-09-02, option C: drop the poisoned issue-159 lane, re-board under a fresh lane id rather than repair it). Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build). The old issue-159 branch/worktree are abandoned in place (permission-denied cleanup, left as garbage per the ruling); the feature diff there (commit 45c76be) is reference-only, re-derive against current main rather than cherry-pick.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  model: fable\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: trait-interface-build\n  issue: gh:174\n  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: mutation-semantics-spike\n  title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: float-build-rust\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Rust backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: float-build-python\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Python backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: float-build-go\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Go backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: float-build-lua\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Lua backend only (host hook), following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: float-build-jq\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the jq backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: float-build-c-escalation\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Attempt Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the C/LLVM backend; if there is no usable native matching primitive, write ESCALATION.md scoped to just this backend, commit it, and stop rather than stalling.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: benchmark-suite-build\n  title: Build the initial benchmark suite -- 8 non-float CLBG tasks, hyperfine harness per benchmark-plan.md, framed as a general cross-language competitiveness claim (gh:147 ruling); n-body/spectral-norm deferred to a second wave (gh:146 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: lines-utf8-posix-research\n  issue: gh:102\n  title: 'Round 2 answer (question lines-non-utf8-byte): no pick -- maintainer wants deeper research beyond plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201 before choosing refuse/carry-through/lossy-replace: what is most correct, most secure, most POSIX-compliant, most common across existing Unix line-oriented tools, and what would match user expectations. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: lines-utf8-edge\n  issue: gh:102\n  title: 'Pin what a non-UTF-8 byte arriving via lines does, on every backend. Round 1 research (plans/string-type-spike.md:194-201) covered per-backend mechanics only; round 2 answer asked for correctness/security/POSIX-compliance/common-practice research first (see lines-utf8-posix-research) before round 3 re-asks with the three options (refuse / carry-where-possible / uniform lossy replacement).'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [lines-utf8-posix-research]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-layer-build\n  title: Wire first/any/all into the general nondeterministic-evaluation model from draft.md#query-is-search (gh:142 ruling, coordinate with search-and-fold-design)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: sort-by-max-by\n  title: sort_by and max_by -- ordering through a scalar projection (euler-ergonomics row 7)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [sum-max-builtins-build]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: param-destructure-build\n  title: Implement match-arm-style parameter destructuring, e.g. fn gcd({a, b}:{a:Int,b:Int}) (gh:144 ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: bigint-tracking\n  issue: gh:112\n  title: Arbitrary-precision integers -- tracked, deliberately unscheduled\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: js-node-web-split-build\n  issue: gh:160\n  title: Build the JS Node/Web target split, TS type generation, builtin-replacement escape hatch, and toylang.conf.yaml as one system (js-node-web-split-design ruling, gh:160)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: module-routing-syntax-build\n  issue: gh:167\n  title: 'Implement `@(path)` module-as-function routing-arm syntax -- ruled (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question module-as-function-routing-design): option C (dedicated routing-arm form) chosen; maintainer refined the spelling from the round''s bare `@path` to parenthesized `@(route-groups/baz.toy)` because it encloses the path expression. See plans/module-routing-research.md.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [file-visibility-tracking-build]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: erlang-target-consideration\n  issue: gh:163\n  title: 'Consider Erlang as an additional backend target, as a design stress test (maintainer note) -- round answer (module-routing-and-erlang-target round, question erlang-target-consideration): option C, spend more research and actually try the Erlang toolchain rather than accepting the desk-research-only finding (plans/erlang-target-research.md). Maintainer also flagged: concurrency will need to be supported by toylang regardless of this backend question -- worth surfacing as its own open item once this research lands.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [erlang-toolchain-empirical-research]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: erlang-toolchain-empirical-research\n  issue: gh:170\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, 2026-09-01; filed as gh:170, a follow-up to gh:163 since the original issue-163 branch is already merged/archived): go beyond plans/erlang-target-research.md''s desk research -- actually install/verify the Erlang toolchain and run the documented-semantics comparison (process/effect model, selective receive, pattern matching) against real `erl`/`erlc` output, not just written docs. Prior attempt (erlang-target-research run 1) hit a sandbox denial trying `which erl`/`erl -eval` and gave up; that incident is logged in plans/opencode-rollout.md -- this is an infrastructure-level restriction, so brief this run to document the denial precisely and stop rather than retry through another channel if it recurs. Write findings as an addendum to plans/erlang-target-research.md.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: variant-checker-capital-first\n  issue: gh:156\n  title: 'Reshaped after land-failure escalation (maintainer: \"reshape into two lanes\", 2026-09-01): checker enforcement (gh:156 ruling) is correct, but landing goes red because the doc corpus still has lowercase-leading variant examples across 6 pages -- gate/xfail the doc-corpus test `every_fragment_is_a_real_program` for exactly these 6 pages (docs/guides/enums.md:13, docs/guides/matching.md:66, docs/reference/operators/comparison.md:28, docs/reference/types/enum.md:7, docs/tutorial/04-enums.md:6/20/40) plus the 4 MISQUOTED fixtures (docs/guides/matching.md:54, docs/reference/types/enum.md:34/67, docs/tutorial/06-matching.md:67) so the checker enforcement itself can land now. The actual doc-content migration is split out as doc-migrate-capital-variants below -- do not do that migration in this lane. Evidence: ~/.cache/toylang-drive/land-gate-issue-156.log.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: doc-migrate-capital-variants\n  title: 'Follow-up lane split from issue-156 (reshape ruling, 2026-09-01): migrate the 6 flagged doc pages'' variant-declaration examples to capital-first-type + lowercase-constructor, and correct the 4 MISQUOTED fixture texts to match the checker''s real error output, then remove the corpus-test gating added in variant-checker-capital-first. Pages: docs/guides/enums.md, docs/guides/matching.md, docs/reference/operators/comparison.md, docs/reference/types/enum.md, docs/tutorial/04-enums.md, docs/tutorial/06-matching.md. Evidence for exact expected strings: ~/.cache/toylang-drive/land-gate-issue-156.log.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [variant-checker-capital-first]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: match-type-wrapper-experiment\n  issue: gh:122\n  title: 'Experimentally build a Match<T> wrapper: `.` entering a `|` chain wraps as Match<T>, traits like `%` get generic impls over Match<T> per underlying T (applying only past a passing boolean guard), and an arm''s aliases become named fields on a result struct the match produces -- see what goes wrong before ratifying (gh:122 ruling)'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: todo\n\n- id: closures-first-class-functions-research\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Research spike (prelude-partials-and-mutation round, Q3): maintainer answer -- both struct-like and array-like partial application are needed, chosen by the input type''s shape, and true currying should fall out for free once closures/functions are first-class rather than being its own mechanism. Look at how Rust and similar languages simplify this before a final design; write findings to plans/'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: partial-application-system-design\n  issue: gh:119\n  title: 'Grill array-like vs struct-like partial application for the colon method-call operator (gh:119 ruling spinoff), informed by closures-first-class-functions-research -- maintainer: \"either five_plus(1) is correct (but still becomes curried) or five_plus [1, 2] is correct; also function will still always only have one parameter; a true curried function would be a function that returns another function; we may want to implement this, or perhaps consider instead thinking about formalizing at least the beginning of a partial application system which could either apply a partial array-like or a partial struct-like\"'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [closures-first-class-functions-research]\n  status: todo\n\n\n\n- id: draft-core-model-migration\n  title: Migrate the two-layer/cardinality core model to a concepts guide; embedded open threads go to the tracker (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  model: fable\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-access-model-migration\n  title: Verify unary functions, lens access, and the dimension-spec model against the reference pages; salvage rationale, delete sections (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-numbers-operators-migration\n  title: Verify the Int/Float/equality sections against ADRs 0006/0007/0010 and the reference; move what's missing, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-records-migration\n  title: Migrate the three record decisions (literals-and-arguments, declared order, order-not-identity) into the record reference, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-streams-migration\n  title: Migrate the five stream/stdin decision sections against ADR 0001, the streams guide, and the sources reference; delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-calls-modules-migration\n  title: Migrate bare application and the prelude module system into the syntax/prelude reference, delete (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-matching-migration\n  title: Migrate the matching/enums sections -- ratified parts to guides and ADR 0009, codec threads to the tracker (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home, matcher-model-clarification, matcher-totality-and-alt-design]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-prototype-findings-migration\n  title: Verify every prototype-showed finding lives in research-log or is superseded, then delete the section (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, draft-questions-home]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-str-adr\n  title: Record the Str contract as an ADR per the string spike's recommendation, then delete the draft's strings section (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  soft: [draft-intro-migration, lines-utf8-edge]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-mutation-migration\n  title: Record the mutation decisions once they land and delete both mutation sections (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [mutation-semantics-design, binary-op-multiplicity-design]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: binary-op-multiplicity-design\n  title: 'Grill binary operators over multi-valued operands (cartesian, zip, or explicit) and the record-forming update''s spelling -- Q2/Q3 (draft-split). Round 2 answers (multiplicity-and-offload-round2): Q3 RATIFIED -- option B, require One on the right of `=`, forking becomes explicit; maintainer caveat: drop the `$c`-prefixed alias in the option''s preview, \"never agreed to prefacing variable names with $\" (applies to whatever alias/binding syntax eventually lands, not specific to this row). Q2 still no pick -- maintainer leans B (zip/broadcast) or C (no default, explicit) but wants to understand how much of jq''s magic/expressive power would be lost either way before choosing; needs jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research before a round 3 re-ask.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: jq-expressiveness-under-zip-or-explicit-research\n  issue: gh:169\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question binary-op-default-multiplicity): survey real jq idioms that rely on the cartesian default for `Vec op Vec` (option A in binary-op-multiplicity-design), and work out concretely what breaks or gets more verbose under a zip/broadcast default (option B) versus no default at all, explicit `zip`/`cross` (option C). Use real jq snippets and their toylang-under-B and toylang-under-C equivalents so the comparison is concrete, not abstract. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-and-fold-design\n  title: 'Grill the search vocabulary (choice, cut, .., ordering) and the applicative fold block -- unratified draft sections (draft-split). Round 1 leaned depth-first for `..` (Q7) but answer was \"also research the performance implications before we fully commit\"; round 2 needed that research before re-asking. Q7 was re-asked in the signature-matching-and-search-cut round and RULED provisional: `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible), flagged for reevaluation once recursive-descent-order-research lands. Round 4 answers (search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, no picks made): Q search-choice-point-syntax -- no pick, maintainer asks whether the choice-point operator should instead be spelled parallel to match syntax rather than a new infix operator; needs search-choice-point-match-syntax-research before re-ask. Q search-cut-semantics -- no pick, maintainer leans \"first should be a real prelude function\" (tentative, not ratified -- re-ask against that framing, i.e. option B reworded as an ordinary stdlib fn rather than compiler-magic sugar). Q applicative-fold-block-syntax -- no pick, maintainer questions whether fold is needed at all (\"fold is basically the same as reduce... reduce is usually too low level, a code smell, results in confusing code\"); before continuing fold syntax design, next round must first ask whether applicative-fold-block-syntax should be justified or dropped.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [search-choice-point-match-syntax-research]\n  soft: [first-search-cut, recursive-descent-order-research]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: recursive-descent-order-research\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, signature-matching-and-search-cut round, question recursive-descent-order): Q7 was ruled provisional -- `..` promises depth-first order (jq-compatible) -- but the maintainer wants a deep research brief on what alternatives actually exist to the unordered/columnar-fast-path design (questions.md Q7), so the ruling can be reevaluated later with real alternatives in hand rather than the two options already on the table. Survey how other query/traversal languages and engines (jq, XPath, JSONPath, columnar/vectorized query engines) handle recursive descent order versus set semantics, and what a toylang-specific unordered fast path would cost callers who need order back. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: search-choice-point-match-syntax-research\n  title: 'Research spike (round answer, search-vocab-choice-cut-fold, question search-choice-point-syntax): maintainer asks for a deeper design pass on whether the choice-point (\"branch wider, explore every alternative\") operator should be spelled parallel to toylang''s match syntax instead of a new infix operator (`;`, `branch(...)`, or folding into `//`). Survey how other languages with pattern-matching-flavored search/logic constructs (Prolog disjunction, Icon/Curry generators, list-monad comprehensions) express this, and produce 2-3 concrete syntax candidates with real toylang code examples, explicitly including a match-parallel option. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: offload-boundary-design\n  title: 'Grill the offload/vectorization thesis -- kernel admissibility, batch invariance, parallel basis, tensors (draft-split). Round 2 answer (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility): silent/cardinality-derived vectorizability CONFIRMED (no type-level effect); the caveat''s discoverability mechanism is B now (opt-in `--explain-offload` compiler diagnostic) with C (editor/LSP hover) as a wanted future addition, not a near-term requirement. Spun off offload-explain-flag-build for B; LSP hover left unscheduled.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [offload-explain-flag-build]\n  soft: [benchmark-goals]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: offload-explain-flag-build\n  issue: gh:168\n  title: 'Implement `--explain-offload`: an opt-in compiler diagnostic that annotates compiled output with which sub-expressions became vectorized kernels and which fell back, and why -- per the offload-boundary-design round 2 ruling (multiplicity-and-offload-round2, question vectorizability-visibility, option B). No signature or type-system change; a build-time flag only. LSP hover (option C) is a wanted future addition, deliberately unscheduled here.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: draft-md-cleanup-review\n  title: Review what remains of draft.md after every split row lands; assign or delete the remainder, retire the file (draft-split)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [draft-questions-home, draft-intro-migration, draft-core-model-migration, draft-access-model-migration, draft-numbers-operators-migration, draft-records-migration, draft-streams-migration, draft-calls-modules-migration, draft-matching-migration, draft-prototype-findings-migration, draft-str-adr, draft-mutation-migration, binary-op-multiplicity-design, search-and-fold-design, offload-boundary-design]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: mail-rounds-isolation\n  issue: gh:164\n  title: Mail tab must not blank on one invalid round file -- per-round fetch isolation + visible error row\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 2\n  status: todo\n\n- id: messagecard-flow-defensive-render\n  issue: gh:173\n  title: 'MessageCard hard-crashes the whole mail/grill UI on an unrecognized flow value (found live: two pending rounds had flow: decide, not a FlowType member, blanking the app). Fall back to a neutral style instead of throwing, and/or add an error boundary around round rendering -- the immediate bad data is already fixed in the two affected round files, this is the defensive follow-up.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 2\n  status: todo\n\n- id: http-query-sugar-design\n  issue: gh:171\n  title: 'Maintainer note (composed 2026-08-31): design minimal HTTP query support (Python-requests-like) as sugar over whatever backend already provides -- needs a grilling round on surface area before it becomes build work'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [http-query-sugar-research]\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: http-query-sugar-research\n  issue: gh:171\n  title: 'Research spike ahead of the http-query-sugar-design round: survey each backend''s existing I/O/effect capabilities (what a source/sink already looks like per backend, and whether any backend has network primitives today) and Python requests'' minimal surface (get/post, headers, body, response shape). Produce 2-3 concrete syntax candidates for HTTP query sugar as toylang code, each grounded in what the current sink/source vocabulary already supports, so the design round can ask a real question instead of a green-field one. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: brief-phrasing-experiment\n  title: 'Tracked in-repo per maintainer ruling (coordinator-escalation-brief-phrasing-experiment round, 2026-09-01: gh issue create was blocked by the permission classifier; no gh issue, no retry). Maintainer note (composed 2026-08-31): generic non-informational reassurance phrasing (\"its much simpler than you think\") in dispatch briefs seems to help planning agents find better plans -- experiment on at least 10 issues, then report progress and grill on the results.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo","lineStart":1,"lineEnd":432,"totalLines":432,"truncated":false}},"title":"plans/board.yaml","time":{"start":1788459736422,"end":1788459736427}},"id":"prt_06881dc08001JVvFyA6avQ15yt","sessionID":"ses_f977ec03affetTdEA9a1zaz0iY","messageID":"msg_06881d3b4001gZkSt03fu6o9BA"}}
