ow a Float\n\n  Line 391:         Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float | Type::Bool => format!(\"String({value})\"),\n\n  Line 566:             | Kind::Float(_)\n\n  Line 775:         Kind::Float(n) => crate::float::lit(*n),\n\n  Line 1056:     if *ty == Type::Float {\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/src/emit_lua.rs:\n  Line 459:         Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n\n  Line 662:             | Kind::Float(_)\n\n  Line 790:         Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/src/emit_py.rs:\n  Line 41: /// Python floors, and `int(a / b)` would truncate through a float and lose bits on the way. The\n\n  Line 374:         Type::Float => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n\n  Line 426:         Kind::Float(_) => unreachable!(\"Float is JS-only in this row\"),\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/src/check/linearity.rs:\n  Line 42:         | Kind::Float(_)\n\n  Line 170:         | Kind::Float(_)\n\n  Line 259:         | Kind::Float(_)\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/src/ast.rs:\n  Line 221:     /// representation every JavaScript engine carries. The dot is what makes a number a Float,\n\n  Line 223:     Float {\n\n  Line 224:         value: f64,\n\n  Line 418:             | Expr::Float { span, .. }\n\n\n/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/src/check/mod.rs:\n  Line 1639:         // A float literal is exactly the binary64 it spells (ADR 0007): there is no width to\n\n  Line 1641:         Expr::Float { value, span } => {\n\n  Line 1643:             Ok(Tir::new(Type::Float, Kind::Float(*value)))\n\n  Line 1852:             if let Expr::Float { value, .. } = base.as_ref() {\n\n  Line 1853:                 return Ok(Tir::new(Type::Float, Kind::Float(-value)));\n\n  Line 1859:                 Ok(inner) if matches!(inner.ty, Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float) => inner,\n\n  Line 2589:         if matches!(left.ty, Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float) {\n\n  Line 2634:         if !matches!(left.ty, Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float) {\n\n  Line 2637:                 format!(\"expected Int, Int64, or Float, found {}\", left.ty),\n\n  Line 2665:         Type::Int | Type::Int64 | Type::Float => {\n","metadata":{"matches":88,"truncated":false},"title":"float|Float|f64","time":{"start":1788459808108,"end":1788459808127}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"So the JS reference is `src/float.rs` which uses `n.to_string()` (Rust's Display for f64). The ADR 0007 says float is IEEE 754 binary64. The issue #149 wants all seven backends to agree byte for byte.\n\nThe existing `lit` function uses Rust's `f64::to_string()` which produces the shortest decimal that round-trips. This is the JS reference behavior since `String(number)` in JS also produces the shortest round-trippable representation.\n\nNow I need to understand the task better. The task is to survey how Go, Python, and Rust each format a binary64 float to string and back, focusing on round-trip precision and hex/exponent edge cases, versus the JS reference. Then write concrete per-backend formatting guidance to plans/.\n\nWait, the brief says \"do not touch plans/\" but also \"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance ... to plans/\". This is the contradiction. Let me look more carefully.\n\nRe-reading the brief one more time:\n\n\"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it -- a partial written finding beats another commitless run.\"\n\n\"Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nOK so these are genuinely contradictory. The most conservative reading: \"do not touch plans/\" is a hard constraint, so I should NOT write to plans/. But then where? The brief says \"a partial written finding beats another commitless run\" — so I must commit something.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider. Maybe \"do not touch plans/\" was meant to be \"do not touch plans/incidents/\" or something else. Or maybe the hard constraint is the real intent and I should write to a different location.\n\nGiven the ESCALATION guidance: \"If something needs deciding that this brief does not settle, write ESCALATION.md at the worktree root -- the question, the alternatives, their costs -- COMMIT it on this branch, take the most conservative continuation, and keep going; never stop to wait for a human.\"\n\nThis brief does settle it — it's a contradiction, but the hard constraints section is explicit and comes last. The most conservative continuation: respect \"do not touch plans/\" and write the findings to a different file. But where? \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The brief says \"do not touch plans/\" — but the whole point of the task (per the issue and the board's \"Write findings to plans/\" pattern used by every other research lane) is to write findings to plans/. \n\nHmm. Let me look at the contradiction differently. The brief says \"Prior 6 runs re-derived context every time and wrote nothing to plans/.\" — this is the FAILURE mode being called out. The task is to NOT repeat that failure. So \"write to plans/\" is the actual intent. The \"do not touch plans/\" in hard constraints might be a typo or copy-paste error.\n\nBut I shouldn't just assume. Let me think about which is more likely a copy-paste error:\n- \"do not touch plans/\" — this is unusual because the task is literally about writing to plans/\n- The \"do NOT push\" is standard\n\nActually, you know what, I bet the \"do not touch plans/\" is a mistake and should have been something else, OR it's a test. Given the ESCALATION guidance says to take \"the most conservative continuation,\" and given that writing nothing is the failure mode explicitly called out, the most conservative continuation that still produces value is:\n\nWrite the findings to a file at the worktree root (e.g., FLOAT-FORMAT-RESEARCH.md or ESCALATION.md), document the contradiction, and commit. This way I respect \"do not touch plans/\" literally while still producing a committed finding.\n\nActually wait — let me re-read one more time even more carefully:\n\n\"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it\"\n\nHmm, maybe I should just trust this and write to plans/. The hard constraint \"do not touch plans/\" might be referring to something else — like don't touch existing plans/ files, only add new ones? No, that's a stretch.\n\nOK here's my decision: I'll write the findings to a new file in plans/ (since that's the explicit task instruction and matches every other research lane's pattern, and the \"wrote nothing to plans/\" failure mode is explicitly called out as bad). The \"do not touch plans/\" hard constraint appears to be a copy-paste error from a different lane's brief (many briefs have \"do not touch X\" constraints that are lane-specific). The issue itself and the board pattern both point to plans/ as the destination.\n\nBut wait — the ESCALATION guidance is clear that when the brief contradicts itself, I should write ESCALATION.md and take the most conservative continuation. The most conservative continuation of \"write to plans/ AND don't touch plans/\" is to NOT write to plans/ but write elsewhere. \n\nHmm, but that defeats the whole task. Let me think about what \"most conservative\" means here. \"Conservative\" = least likely to cause harm / least assumption. Writing to plans/ when told \"do not touch plans/\" violates a hard constraint. Writing elsewhere respects the hard constraint. So the most conservative continuation is to write elsewhere.\n\nBut then the task's main deliverable (findings in plans/) isn't met. The brief explicitly says \"a partial written finding beats another commitless run\" — so a committed finding anywhere is better than nothing.\n\nOK, final decision: I'll write ESCALATION.md documenting the contradiction, AND write the research findings to a file at the worktree root (FLOAT-FORMAT-FINDINGS.md or similar — NOT in plans/), commit both. This respects the hard constraint literally while still delivering a committed finding. This is the most conservative continuation.\n\nWait, actually, let me reconsider one final time. Let me look at the exact wording again:\n\n\"do not touch plans/.\"\n\nHmm. What if \"plans/\" here refers to the plans/ directory being off-limits because it's managed by the coordinator/board, and the findings should go... somewhere else? But the brief says \"to plans/\" explicitly for the findings.\n\nYou know what, I think the cleanest interpretation that respects BOTH the letter of the hard constraint AND the spirit of the task: the contradiction is real, write ESCALATION.md, and write the findings to a non-plans/ location. The maintainer can then move it to plans/ if that was the intent, or the ESCALATION documents the contradiction for them to resolve.\n\nLet me go with: ESCALATION.md + FLOAT-FORMAT-FINDINGS.md at worktree root. Both committed.\n\nActually, hmm, wait. Let me reconsider whether I'm overthinking this. Let me re-read the FULL brief one more time, focusing on the exact contradiction.\n\n\"Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it -- a partial written finding beats another commitless run. While iterating, test with `just check` (the fast loop); `just --list` names every repo task. Definition of done: implementation complete; `just check` green from the worktree root (a cold worktree compiles first -- give it time, never abort it; the full `just test` runs at landing, not per lane); `just fmt` and `just clippy` clean on code you touched; work committed on this branch per AGENTS.md with the provenance line \"Written by DeepSeek V4 Flash via opencode.\". Hard constraints: work ONLY inside this worktree; do NOT push; do not touch plans/.\"\n\nOK so \"implementation complete\" is in the definition of done. What implementation? The task is research/survey, not implementation of float in the backends. Unless... the \"implementation\" refers to implementing the formatting guidance in the actual backends (emit_go.rs, emit_py.rs, emit_rs.rs)?\n\nWait, re-reading: \"Survey how Go ... Python ... and Rust ... each format an IEEE binary64 float to a string and back versus the already-landed JS reference, focusing on round-trip precision and hex/exponent edge cases. ... Write concrete per-backend formatting guidance (with short verified code snippets) to plans/ and commit it\"\n\nSo the task is: SURVEY (research) + WRITE GUIDANCE to plans/. The \"implementation complete\" in the definition of done might refer to implementing the guidance in the actual emit_*.rs files? Or it might just mean the research/writing is complete.\n\nHmm, but \"just check green from the worktree root\" and \"just fmt and just clippy clean on code you touched\" — this implies I might touch source code. If I'm only writing a markdown file to plans/, there's no code to fmt/clippy. So \"implementation complete\" + \"code you touched\" suggests I should also implement the float formatting in the backends.\n\nBut wait — there are already separate lanes for that: float-build-rust, float-build-python, float-build-go (all status: delegated). This lane (issue-float-format-research) is the RESEARCH lane that those build lanes depend on. Let me check — float-build-rust needs: [float-build]. But there's no \"float-build\" lane in the board, only float-build-{rust,python,go,lua,jq,c-escalation}. Hmm, the needs reference \"float-build\" which doesn't exist as a lane. Maybe \"float-build\" is the JS reference lane that's already landed.\n\nActually, looking at the board more carefully: float-build-rust needs [float-build], float-build-python needs [float-build], float-build-go needs [float-build]. But there's no lane with id \"float-build\". The JS implementation is already landed (it's in src/float.rs and emit_js.rs). So \"float-build\" might refer to the already-landed JS implementation, or it might be this research lane.\n\nOK, I think the task is primarily RESEARCH: survey the three languages' float formatting, write guidance to plans/. The \"implementation complete\" and \"code you touched\" might be boilerplate from the brief template. But if I do touch code (e.g., to verify snippets), I should ensure fmt/clippy are clean.\n\nActually, the brief says \"with short verified code snippets\" — so I need to verify code snippets. I can do that by running small programs in Go, Python, and Rust. But wait — the brief says I can't use `cargo run` or direct binary execution. I can run `python3 script.py` after writing with the write tool. For Go and Rust, I need to find allowed alternatives.\n\nHmm, let me check what's available. Let me look at the emit_*.rs files to understand the backend structure, and check if there are existing float-related tests.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about what I can actually DO here:\n\n1. Read the existing JS reference (float.rs — done, it uses Rust's f64::to_string())\n2. Read the emit_*.rs files to understand how each backend emits floats\n3. Survey Go, Python, Rust float formatting behavior (from public knowledge + verification)\n4. Write guidance to plans/ (or elsewhere given the contradiction)\n\nFor verification, I can:\n- Python: write a script and run `python3 script.py`\n- Go: can I run `go run`? Let me check if go is available. The brief says no direct binary execution like `cargo run -- run some_file.toy` or `./target/debug/toylang`. But `go run` for a standalone Go file might be OK? Actually, the brief says \"direct binary execution such as `cargo run -- run some_file.toy` or `./target/debug/toylang ...` (never runnable, no exceptions)\". This seems to be about toylang's own binary, not about running Go/Python/Rust verification scripts. Let me try.\n- Rust: I can write a small Rust program and... hmm, `cargo run` might be blocked. But I could potentially use `rustc` directly? Or maybe I can add a test case to the corpus.\n\nActually, the cleanest way to verify Rust float formatting: it's already what `src/float.rs` uses (`n.to_string()`). So Rust's behavior IS the reference. I just need to document it.\n\nFor Go and Python, I can write small scripts and run them. Let me check if `go` and `python3` are available.\n\nLet me also look at the emit_*.rs files to understand the current state of float emission in each backend.\n\nLet me proceed step by step. First, let me look at the emit files for float handling and check what tools are available.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_06882f3db001lK80b5heVBkBsU","sessionID":"ses_f977ec03affetTdEA9a1zaz0iY","messageID":"msg_068820bc4001COtF4G89IrJhOh"}}
